===Etymology===
Borrowed from {{bor|en|nl|polder}}, from {{der|en|dum|polre}}, from {{der|en|odt|polra}}, of uncertain origin.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈpəʊldə/|/ˈpɒldə/|a=UK}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-polder.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{hyphenation|en|pol|der}}
* {{rhymes|en|əʊldə(ɹ)|s=2}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun}}

# {{lb|en|geography}} An area of ground reclaimed from a sea or lake by means of [[dike]]s. {{defdate|from 17th c.}}
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1999|author=Geert Mak|translator=w:Philipp Blom|title=Amsterdam: A Brief Life of the City|page=43|publisher=Vintage|year_published=2001
|passage=The patron saint of the Oude Kerk, Saint Nicolaas, the ‘water saint’, was also very popular, as he protected the sailors and those living on the '''polders''' from the dangers of the sea.}}

